>
  <p>
    The acceleration due to gravity at Earth's surface—approximately 9.8 m/s²—is one of the most frequently used
    values in physics. But this value isn't constant everywhere, and measuring it precisely reveals surprising
    information about Earth's interior, tides, groundwater, and even how continents move.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Gravitational Acceleration?</h2>
  <p>
    Near Earth's surface, all objects fall with the same acceleration (ignoring air resistance). This acceleration,
    called g, varies slightly with location due to Earth's rotation, altitude, latitude, and local mass variations.
    Standard gravity is defined as exactly 9.80665 m/s², though actual values range from about 9.78 to 9.83 m/s².
  </p>

  <h3>Why g Varies</h3>
  <p>
    Several factors cause g to vary:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Latitude: Earth's rotation creates centrifugal effect, weakening effective gravity at the equator</li>
    <li>Altitude: Gravity weakens with height above sea level</li>
    <li>Local geology: Dense rocks or mineral deposits increase g; caves or less dense material decrease it</li>
    <li>Tides: The Moon and Sun's gravitational pull slightly changes g throughout the day</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Classical Methods</h2>

  <h3>Free Fall Measurement</h3>
  <p>
    The most direct method: drop an object and measure how fast it accelerates. Modern versions use laser
    interferometry to track a falling object's position to millimeter precision, determining g to within one part
    in a billion. These absolute gravimeters are the gold standard for precise measurements.
  </p>

  <h3>The Simple Pendulum</h3>
  <p>
    A pendulum's period depends on g through the formula T = 2π√(L/g), where T is period and L is length. By
    timing many swings of a pendulum of known length, you can calculate g. This method was used historically
    and works well for educational demonstrations, achieving about 1% accuracy with careful technique.
  </p>

  <h3>The Kater Pendulum</h3>
  <p>
    This sophisticated reversible pendulum, invented in 1817, can measure g to 0.001% accuracy. It uses two pivot
    points and relies on the fact that if the pendulum has the same period when suspended from either pivot, the
    period formula simplifies. Historical surveys used these instruments extensively.
  </p>

  <h2>Modern Gravimeters</h2>

  <h3>Spring Gravimeters</h3>
  <p>
    These instruments use a mass suspended by a very sensitive spring. Changes in gravity cause tiny changes in
    spring length, measured by optical or electronic sensors. While not absolute (they measure changes, not
    absolute values), they're portable and can detect variations of one part in 10 million.
  </p>

  <h3>Superconducting Gravimeters</h3>
  <p>
    These ultra-sensitive instruments levitate a superconducting sphere in a magnetic field. Changes in gravity
    cause the sphere to move, detected with extreme precision. They can measure variations smaller than one part
    in a billion, recording tidal effects, atmospheric pressure changes, and even seismic waves.
  </p>

  <h3>Atomic Gravimeters</h3>
  <p>
    The newest technology uses atom interferometry—allowing atoms to fall in quantum superposition states and
    measuring how their matter waves interfere. These portable quantum sensors achieve absolute measurements
    rivaling laboratory instruments and represent the future of gravimetry.
  </p>

  <h2>Applications of Precise Gravity Measurements</h2>

  <h3>Resource Exploration</h3>
  <p>
    Mining and oil companies use gravity surveys to locate underground resources. Dense ore bodies or oil-bearing
    rock formations create detectable gravity anomalies. Airborne and satellite gravimetry can map large regions
    efficiently.
  </p>

  <h3>Groundwater Monitoring</h3>
  <p>
    Changes in groundwater levels alter local gravity measurably. Continuous monitoring can track aquifer depletion
    or recharge without drilling wells. This non-invasive technique is increasingly important for water resource
    management.
  </p>

  <h3>Volcano Monitoring</h3>
  <p>
    Magma movement beneath volcanoes changes the local gravitational field. Continuous gravity monitoring helps
    predict eruptions by detecting subsurface magma redistribution before surface deformation becomes obvious.
  </p>

  <h3>Geodesy and Earth Structure</h3>
  <p>
    Precise gravity measurements reveal Earth's internal structure—from crust thickness to mantle convection
    patterns. They help determine the geoid (Earth's true shape accounting for gravity variations) crucial for
    accurate GPS elevation and satellite orbit prediction.
  </p>

  <h2>Satellite Gravimetry</h2>
  <p>
    Satellites like GRACE (Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment) measure Earth's gravity field from space by
    precisely tracking orbital perturbations. GRACE detected:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Ice sheet mass loss in Greenland and Antarctica</li>
    <li>Groundwater depletion in major aquifers</li>
    <li>Post-glacial rebound (land rising after ice sheet removal)</li>
    <li>Ocean current changes affecting gravity through water redistribution</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Gravity of Time</h2>
  <p>
    Gravitational time dilation means clocks run at different rates in different gravitational fields. Modern
    atomic clocks are so precise that moving them vertically by centimeters changes their tick rate measurably.
    This enables "chronometric leveling"—using clocks as altimeters and gravity sensors simultaneously.
  </p>

  <h2>Amateur Measurements</h2>
  <p>
    You can measure g at home with surprising accuracy:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Pendulum: Time 100 swings of a measured-length pendulum for ~1% accuracy</li>
    <li>Free fall: Drop objects past video camera frames for ~5% accuracy</li>
    <li>Inclined plane: Roll objects down measured inclines, timing carefully</li>
    <li>Smartphone accelerometers: Apps can measure g to ~0.1 m/s² accuracy</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Future Developments</h2>
  <p>
    Next-generation gravity measurement will combine:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Portable quantum gravimeters for field surveys</li>
    <li>Continuous satellite monitoring at higher resolution</li>
    <li>Dense networks of permanent gravity stations</li>
    <li>AI and machine learning for interpreting complex gravity data</li>
  </ul>

